          "question": "Got stung by a wasp an hour ago, my hand has ballooned and now my eyelids look puffy and I feel strange.",
          "answer": "**Seek emergency care now.** Call your local emergency number, or have someone take you to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait to see whether it settles.\n\nThe tool's rule table did not match this description and returned \"routine.\" That is not a clearance. A swollen hand and puffy eyelids within an hour of a sting, plus feeling strange, can be signs of a serious allergic reaction. Guidance says to suspect anaphylaxis when symptoms begin within minutes to a couple of hours of an exposure and involve swelling, dizziness, or faintness ([red-flag-anaphylaxis]). I am raising the urgency above the tool's tier because missing a dangerous reaction is the worse error.\n\nCall emergency services immediately, and do not drive yourself, if you notice any of these:\n\n- Swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, or a tight throat\n- Trouble swallowing, a hoarse voice, or noisy breathing\n- Shortness of breath, wheezing, or chest tightness\n- Dizziness, faintness, confusion, or feeling like you might pass out\n- Hives or flushing spreading across the body\n- Vomiting or severe stomach pain\n\nIf you have an emergency allergy kit that a clinician has prescribed to you, follow the instructions you were given for it. Lie down with your legs raised if you feel faint, and do not stand up quickly. Tell the emergency team about the sting and the time it happened.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
